               <packagedElement xmi:id="cdi.Category"
                                 xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#Category"
                                 xmi:type="uml:Class">
                  <ownedComment xmi:id="cdi.Category-ownedComment"
                                 xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#Category-ownedComment"
                                 xmi:type="uml:Comment">
                     <body>Definition 
============ 
Concept whose role is to define and measure a characteristic.  

Examples 
========== 
The category "Male" is used to define or measure "Sex" or "Gender", which are characteristics. In turn, they are also variables. "Extremely Satisfied" is a category in an agreement scale. This can be used for many kinds of variables.  


Explanatory notes 
=================== 
A category is a concept, typically associated with a code in the representation of a variable value. Categories are most often used in the allowed values for qualitative, i.e., nominal and ordinal, variables. A set of categories, say "Male" and "Female" for characteristics "Sex" or "Gender," helps define those characteristics. For describing location of measurement station, you might have categories "Urban", "Suburban", "Rural", etc.
</body>
                     <annotatedElement xmi:idref="cdi.Category"/>
                  </ownedComment>
                  <name>Category</name>
                  <generalization xmi:id="cdi.Category-generalization"
                                   xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#Category-generalization"
                                   xmi:type="uml:Generalization">
                     <general xmi:idref="cdi.Concept"/>
                  </generalization>
                  <ownedAttribute xmi:id="cdi.Category-descriptiveText"
                                   xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#Category-descriptiveText"
                                   xmi:type="uml:Property">
                     <name>descriptiveText</name>
                     <type xmi:idref="cdi.InternationalString"/>
                     <lowerValue xmi:id="cdi.Category-descriptiveText-lowerValue"
                                  xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#Category-descriptiveText-lowerValue"
                                  xmi:type="uml:LiteralInteger">
                        <value>0</value>
                     </lowerValue>
                  </ownedAttribute>
               </packagedElement>
               <packagedElement xmi:id="cdi.CategoryPosition"
                                 xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#CategoryPosition"
                                 xmi:type="uml:Class">
                  <ownedComment xmi:id="cdi.CategoryPosition-ownedComment"
                                 xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#CategoryPosition-ownedComment"
                                 xmi:type="uml:Comment">
                     <body>Definition
============
Assigns a sequence number to a category within a list.
</body>
                     <annotatedElement xmi:idref="cdi.CategoryPosition"/>
                  </ownedComment>
                  <name>CategoryPosition</name>
                  <ownedAttribute xmi:id="cdi.CategoryPosition-identifier"
                                   xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#CategoryPosition-identifier"
                                   xmi:type="uml:Property">
                     <name>identifier</name>
                     <type xmi:idref="cdi.Identifier"/>
                     <lowerValue xmi:id="cdi.CategoryPosition-identifier-lowerValue"
                                  xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#CategoryPosition-identifier-lowerValue"
                                  xmi:type="uml:LiteralInteger">
                        <value>0</value>
                     </lowerValue>
                  </ownedAttribute>
                  <ownedAttribute xmi:id="cdi.CategoryPosition-value"
                                   xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#CategoryPosition-value"
                                   xmi:type="uml:Property">
                     <name>value</name>
                     <type xmi:type="uml:PrimitiveType"
                            href="http://www.eclipse.org/uml2/5.0.0/UML/PrimitiveTypes.xmi#Integer"/>
                  </ownedAttribute>
                  <ownedAttribute xmi:id="cdi.CategoryPosition-ownedAttribute-3"
                                   xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#CategoryPosition-ownedAttribute-3"
                                   xmi:type="uml:Property">
                     <isReadOnly>true</isReadOnly>
                     <type xmi:idref="cdi.Category"/>
                     <association xmi:idref="cdi.CategoryPosition_indexes_Category"/>
                  </ownedAttribute>
               </packagedElement>
               <packagedElement xmi:id="cdi.CategoryRelationship"
                                 xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#CategoryRelationship"
                                 xmi:type="uml:Class">
                  <ownedComment xmi:id="cdi.CategoryRelationship-ownedComment"
                                 xmi:uuid="https://www.ddialliance.org/Specification/DDI-CDI/1.0/#CategoryRelationship-ownedComment"
                                 xmi:type="uml:Comment">
                     <body>Definition 
============ 
Source-to-target relationship between categories in a structure.   

Examples 
========== 
In the International Standard Industrial Classification of All Economic Activities (ISIC) Revision 4 (https://unstats.un.org/unsd/demographic-social/census/documents/isic_rev4.pdf), the super-type/sub-type relation is used throughout. The super-type "Manufacturing Sector" has among its sub-types "Manufacture of Rubber and Plastics Products" (division 22). In this example, the super-type is the source (Manufacturing Sector") and the sub-type is the target. The semantics attribute will provide the type of the relationship.

Explanatory notes 
=================== 
Relationships between pairs of categories are linkages between them. The meaning of the linkage is determined by the relation.</body>
