                     <body>Definition 
============ 
Set of relationships between the members within or between statistical classifications.  

Examples 
========== 
Correspondence between the U.S. Standard Industrial Classification (SIC) and North American Industrial Classification System (NAICS).  

Explanatory notes 
=================== 
Correspondence tables are used with statistical classifications. For instance, it can relate two versions from the same classification series; statistical classifications from different classification series; a variant and the version on which it is based; or different versions of a variant. In the first and last examples, the correspondence table facilitates comparability over time.</body>
